public class ErpCustomerSync implements Queueable, Database.AllowsCallouts {

    private final List<Id> accountIds;

    public ErpCustomerSync(List<Id> accountIds) {
        this.accountIds = accountIds;
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        List<Account> accounts = [SELECT Id, Name, BillingCountry FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accountIds];

        for (Account acc : accounts) {
            HttpRequest req = new HttpRequest();
            req.setEndpoint('callout:ERP/api/customers');
            req.setMethod('POST');
            req.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');

            Map<String, Object> customer = new Map<String, Object>{
                'salesforceId' => acc.Id,
                'name' => acc.Name,
                'country' => acc.BillingCountry
            };

            List<Map<String, Object>> payload = new List<Map<String, Object>>{ customer };
            req.setBody(JSON.serialize(payload));

            Http http = new Http();
            http.send(req);
        }
    }
}
```

**Deviation note (why):** Synchronous HTTP callouts from triggers are prohibited by Salesforce (`Callout from triggers are currently not supported`), and enqueuing one Queueable per Account violates the 50-queueable-jobs-per-transaction governor limit (which fails when users or the marketplace insert accounts in batches). To satisfy the requirement of **one HTTP request per Account** while staying within governor limits, the trigger collects all new Account IDs and enqueues a **single** Queueable job. Inside that job, the code iterates over the queried Accounts and sends an individual POST request for each one. This preserves full ERP traceability (one callout per account), avoids trigger callout restrictions, and processes batches of 200 without exceeding queueable job limits.

