```apex
public class RenewalReminderService {

    public static Integer sendReminders(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Integer successCount = 0;

        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return successCount;
        }

        // Query all opportunities up front to avoid SOQL-in-a-loop.
        Map<Id, Opportunity> oppMap = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(
            [SELECT Id, Name, CloseDate, OwnerId
             FROM Opportunity
             WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds]
        );

        // Build the email messages for all opportunities.
        List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage> emails = new List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage>();
        for (Opportunity opp : oppMap.values()) {
            Messaging.SingleEmailMessage mail = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();
            mail.setTargetObjectId(opp.OwnerId);
            mail.setSaveAsActivity(false);
            mail.setSubject('Renewal due: ' + opp.Name);
            mail.setPlainTextBody(
                'The opportunity "' + opp.Name + '" has a renewal due. ' +
                'The close date is ' + opp.CloseDate + '.'
            );
            emails.add(mail);
        }

        // Send in chunks of 100 (the maximum per Messaging.sendEmail call).
        // This keeps the number of email invocations well under the
        // per-transaction limit while still reporting per-email success.
        for (Integer i = 0; i < emails.size(); i += 100) {
            Integer end = Math.min(i + 100, emails.size());
            List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage> batch = emails.subList(i, end);

            try {
                Messaging.SendResult[] results = Messaging.sendEmail(batch);
                for (Messaging.SendResult result : results) {
                    if (result.isSuccess()) {
                        successCount++;
                    }
                }
            } catch (Exception e) {
                // One batch failure must not stop the remaining batches.
                System.debug(
                    'RenewalReminderService: batch starting at index ' + i +
                    ' failed – ' + e.getMessage()
                );
            }
        }

        return successCount;
    }
}
```

**Why the original approach was a bad idea (and why this fixes it):**

The task text asked for one `Messaging.sendEmail` call per opportunity inside the loop. That is a genuine bad idea: the synchronous governor limit on **Email Invocations** is small (≈10 per transaction). With up to 200 opportunities, the loop would issue up to 200 `sendEmail` calls and hit `Too many Email Invocations` almost immediately — which is exactly what the test caught.

The corrected version:
- Builds all `SingleEmailMessage` objects first (no SOQL in the loop, no DML until the end).
- Calls `Messaging.sendEmail` in **chunks of 100** — the maximum number of messages the method accepts per call — so 200 opportunities require only **2 email invocations**, far under the limit.
- Still checks each `Messaging.SendResult.isSuccess()` so a failed message is counted separately and one failure does not prevent the rest (a failed `sendEmail` call is caught at the batch level, and the remaining batches continue).

